      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "A close quinazolinone analog reached 5.5 µg/g in mouse brain after intraperitoneal administration, directly demonstrating meaningful brain exposure after systemic dosing.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "Tacrine, a structurally similar lipophilic drug, was rapidly taken up into rat brain and localized to cortex, hippocampus, thalamus, and striatum by quantitative whole-body autoradiography, supporting meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Cryptolepine was not detected in brain tissue at any time point, indicating that at least one close analog lacks BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Most structurally similar analogs show measurable brain exposure after systemic administration, including direct brain concentrations and autoradiographic brain uptake; one impermeable analog and some low-penetration records reduce certainty.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-molecule CNS exposure data are available; nearest analogs have modest Morgan similarity (0.25–0.30).",
    "One high-similarity card appears to describe M10/M30 rather than the listed analog, creating a provenance conflict.",
    "Some positive records are qualitative or from disease models, and a few analogs show poor or low brain penetration, so transfer is not uniform."
  ],
